  ],
  "title": "Powers, Roots & Standard Form",
  "content": "<p><strong>Powers</strong> (indices) tell you how many times to multiply a number by itself: \\(a^n = a \\times a \\times \\ldots\\) (\\(n\\) times). Key rules: \\(a^m \\times a^n = a^{m+n}\\), \\(a^m \\div a^n = a^{m-n}\\), \\((a^m)^n = a^{mn}\\), \\(a^0 = 1\\), \\(a^{-n} = \\frac{1}{a^n}\\).</p><p><strong>Roots</strong> are the inverse of powers: \\(\\sqrt{a} = a^{1/2}\\), \\(\\sqrt[3]{a} = a^{1/3}\\). Fractional indices: \\(a^{m/n} = (\\sqrt[n]{a})^m\\).</p><p><strong>Standard form</strong> writes very large or small numbers as \\(A \\times 10^n\\) where \\(1 \\leq A < 10\\) and \\(n\\) is an integer. For large numbers \\(n > 0\\), for small numbers \\(n < 0\\). To convert, move the decimal point until A is between 1 and 10, and count the moves to find n.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Write</strong> \\(0.00034\\) in standard form</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Move point right until A is between 1 and 10: \\(3.4\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Count moves: 4 places right → \\(n = -4\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(3.4 \\times 10^{-4}\\)</p>"
 },
